{"title":"Pell equation","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>A <b>Pell equation</b> is a type of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Diophantine_equation\" title=\"Diophantine equation\">diophantine equation</a> in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/6/e9628863220470b09384327a3d32789f43caed56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2-Dy^2 = \\pm1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"119\" height=\"18\" /> for a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Natural_number\" title=\"Natural number\">natural number</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. Generally, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is taken to be square-free, since otherwise we can \"absorb\" the largest square factor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/4/a94449ba62cd5d570da7cfbc270323d4c67182fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d^2 | D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"21\" /> into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> by setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/b/64bfecfc01f25b35f8034531e4d0f041c0edf701.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y&#39; = dy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"57\" height=\"17\" />. \n</p><p>Note that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/3/2b3e1ca63b624be3008604edc8ca4aa503d551f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D = d^2$\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" /> is a perfect square, then this problem can be solved using <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Difference_of_squares\" title=\"Difference of squares\">difference of squares</a>. We would have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/e/12e4ba0b8893dd56358de87d9e2539b53761217a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 - Dy^2 = (x+dy)(x-dy) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"260\" height=\"19\" />, from which we can use casework to quickly determine the solutions. \n</p><p>Alternatively, if D is a nonsquare then there are infinitely many distinct solutions to the pell equation. To prove this it must first be shown that there is a single solution to the pell equation.\n</p><p>Claim: If D is a positive integer that is not a perfect square, then the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/4/6c46d428f6bf974a7b4117b823751e33d39f323a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2-Dy^2 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"105\" height=\"18\" /> has a solution in positive integers.\n</p><p>Proof: Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/0/ba09e029e54f3f870b2f3256b1a02d3994fc7a67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be an integer greater than 1. We will show that there exists integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab09eb0e14820db6dabcda6e9cc8638044351ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/a/3ba100c3817fb7eacfe4bfce54bf701adc16f563.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/8/b38cf92825a7946332385a9febbc7e335e514057.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{1}-w_{1}\\sqrt{D} &lt; \\frac{1}{c_{1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"129\" height=\"39\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/7/e97f544758d5dd56eb0eeda688c19a093bc1497b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w_{1} \\le c_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" />. Consider the sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/1/d51b063015e0da1b4a72b907c4f61efa8a41e1cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l_{k} = [k\\sqrt{D}+1] \\rightarrow 0 \\le l_{k}-k\\sqrt{d} \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"284\" height=\"22\" />   <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/6/536ba5a392431e74fec5a8db2257504c85fcc206.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\forall$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" />   <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/c/2dc40696cbe20fa0b020ab776bcd391e9fe59bec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\le k \\le c_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"81\" height=\"15\" />. By the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pigeonhole_Principle\" title=\"Pigeonhole Principle\">Pigeonhole Principle</a> it can be seen that there exists i, j, and p such that i &lt; j, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/1/8e1f4ca9ef88f4f37a4402d999a2c10963e9f11a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\le i, j, p, \\le c_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"113\" height=\"16\" />  and \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/a/78a0ee32fe7b410462ae551f880e42fcd6f06744.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p-1}{c_{1}} &lt; l_{i}-i\\sqrt{D} \\le \\frac{p}{c_{1}}, \\frac{p-1}{c_{1}} &lt; l_{j}-j\\sqrt{D} \\le \\frac{p}{c_{1}}\\rightarrow (l_{j}-l_{i})-(j-i)\\sqrt{D} &lt; \\frac{1}{c_{1}} \\rightarrow t_{1} = l_{j}-l_{i}, w_{1} = j-i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"813\" height=\"39\" />.\n</p><p>So we now have \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/7/db7cbee0a914e7413afbd80900a92b4f76e83f2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{1}-w_{1}\\sqrt{D} &lt; \\frac{1}{c_{1}} \\rightarrow t_{1}+w_{1}\\sqrt{D} &lt; 2w_{1}\\sqrt{D}+1\\rightarrow t_{1}^2-Dw_{1}^2 &lt; 2\\frac{w_{1}}{c_{1}}\\sqrt{D}+\\frac{1}{c_{1}}&lt;2\\sqrt{D}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"688\" height=\"39\" />.\n</p><p>We can now create a sequence of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/5/7055cd0cf88a8a55b2b2105c57d6ef13e4280ef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{n}, w_{n}, c_{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"70\" height=\"15\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/e/09ec25792bcdcb73e96cc9278e71b4cd24d522d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{n}-w_{n}\\sqrt{D} &lt; \\frac{1}{c_{n}}, t_{n}^2-Dw_{n}^2 &lt; 2\\sqrt{D}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"313\" height=\"39\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/d/cbdcb5486acc977f249ae4857ab86e4c3223da3c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{n}-w_{n}\\sqrt{D} &gt; \\frac{1}{c_{n+1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"151\" height=\"41\" /> which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/9/559a220c4ce285a40911cd36573bd6073c612dff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{s} \\not= t_{r}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/6/536ba5a392431e74fec5a8db2257504c85fcc206.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\forall$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> r and s. However we can see by the pigeon hole principle that there is another infinite sequence which will be denoted by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/5/085257c700319f40eefa59b4e3b27cf444757b0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{y_{k}}, w_{y_{k}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"16\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/c/92c6d1e5a200ba55768631e5318a234efe988330.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{y_{k}}^2-Dw_{y_{k}}^2 = h &lt; 2\\sqrt{D}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"216\" height=\"24\" />. Once again, from the pigeon hole principle we can see that there exist integers f and g such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/7/9e7981538bbb7d8c21dc22797dad77fdacbda40a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_{y_{f}}^2-Dw_{y_{f}}^2 = t_{y_{g}}^2-Dw_{y_{g}}^2 = H, t_{y_{f}} = t_{y_{g}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -8px\" width=\"310\" height=\"23\" /> mod H, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/f/6bf2a1d8306962b3b84c75556d8a8bb32e2617b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w_{y_{f}} = w_{y_{g}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"77\" height=\"14\" /> mod H, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/3/cd3e3ca04e395d769e122638ae918e5bbba0aa1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{t_{y_{f}}}{w_{y_{f}}} \\not= \\frac{t_{y_{g}}}{w_{y_{g}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"86\" height=\"43\" />. Define <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/f/07f82a43862f43501431e31fab5ed7df3c9262c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = t_{y_{f}}t_{y_{g}}-Dw_{y_{f}}w_{y_{g}}, Y = t_{y_{f}}w_{y_{g}}-t_{y_{g}}w_{y_{f}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"341\" height=\"18\" /> and notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/b/f8b5526143c27464f47a2b66deb4c16028901d8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X^2-DY^2 = H^2$\" width=\"130\" height=\"15\" />. Also note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/7/bd7baaf6c8972088308fdb21adc80ecf07f9e3b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = t_{y_{f}}t_{y_{g}}-Dw_{y_{f}}w_{y_{g}} = t_{y_{f}}^2-Dw_{y_{f}}^2 = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -8px\" width=\"321\" height=\"23\" /> mod H which means that Y = 0 mod H also. We can now see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/2/f7212c6591025fd04512ab1a01ed37b87d7d80ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{X}{H}, \\frac{Y}{H}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"47\" height=\"37\" /> is a nontrivial solution to pell's equation.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Family_of_solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Family of solutions</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Continued_fractions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Continued fractions</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Generalization\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Generalization</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Introductory_Problems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Introductory Problems</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Intermediate_Problems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Intermediate Problems</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Family_of_solutions\">Family of solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pell_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Family of solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e3648ce4903aa24b129162eeaec2330fed4645.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_{0}, y_{0})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> be the minimal solution to the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/4/6c46d428f6bf974a7b4117b823751e33d39f323a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2-Dy^2 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"105\" height=\"18\" />. Note that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/d/ebdeeddb703943227a7d341ebf0d73b159c77082.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a,b), (c, d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" /> are solutions to this equation then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/0/d00d3851e121a638f8940a78cef8d169737d5d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a^2-Db^2)(c^2-Dd^2) = (ac+Dbd)^2-D(cb+ad)^2 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"435\" height=\"19\" /> which means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c51456a927474288a9e46eee421946318729ac8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ac+Dbd, cb+ad)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"150\" height=\"18\" /> is another solution. From this we can guess that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/6/3c6b7a5413ac9a0eddbf5880f55794461318d590.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_{n}, y_{n})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> is obtained from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/5/2c5ed5f0b11205b4d7be0f1c30914f086395e816.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_{0}^2-Dy_{0}^2)^{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"110\" height=\"19\" />. This does indeed generate all the solutions to this equation. Assume there was another solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/4/e148d95e9a2349eff0d0ccd1ec50ace5fa25269b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(p, q)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/b/b6b7a40ab0d8178e83fe1b91c511d04b1246059b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(p,q)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> is non-minimal, then there exists some integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> such that\n</p>\n<pre><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/7/1979aa19dd67c77a351fd718b73ab27c7777495c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{m}+\\sqrt{D}y_{m} &lt; p+\\sqrt{D}q &lt; x_{m+1}+\\sqrt{D}y_{m+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"346\" height=\"21\" />\n</pre>\n<p>Next, multiply the inequality by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/f/9affe1a20392aac03a3b3fcca46b740bdd5aefe2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{m}-\\sqrt{D}y_{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"96\" height=\"21\" /> to obtain:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/b/e5b1d26b0d320eeae165fada35d3f3195b16b91f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 &lt; (p+\\sqrt{D}q)(x_{m}-\\sqrt{D}y_{m}) = (px_{m}-Dqy_{m})+\\sqrt{D}(qx_{m}-py_{m})&lt; x_{0}+y_{0}\\sqrt{D}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"632\" height=\"22\" />. \n</p><p>However, it can be seen that \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/e/1ae2819c53287870e9886f242f7335ffdaabba64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(px_{m}-Dqy_{m})^2-D(qx_{m}-py_{m})^2 = (p+\\sqrt{D}q)(x_{m}-\\sqrt{D}y_{m})(p-\\sqrt{D}q)(x_{m}+\\sqrt{D}y_{m}) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"715\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p>Meaning <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/7/b27090f98498cd3fd62ab72d1e03a0dec77924ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(px_{m}-Dqy_{m}, qx_{m}-py_{m})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"205\" height=\"18\" /> is a solution smaller than the minimal solution which is a contradiction.\n</p><p>Therefore, such <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/b/b6b7a40ab0d8178e83fe1b91c511d04b1246059b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(p,q)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> cannot exist and so the method of composition generates every possible solution to Pell's equation.\n</p><p>For a Pell equation in form of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/4/6c46d428f6bf974a7b4117b823751e33d39f323a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2-Dy^2 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"105\" height=\"18\" />, its roots are in the form of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/1/8615f86d1fa69e790994196aa98b6a382317586a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_{0} + \\sqrt{D}y_0)^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"111\" height=\"24\" />, in which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/e/41e669253c4e7faee3f83a492830f13b68c47b98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/e/9bed9ab9e3bc69f63f6469dce000f0d83c3fa38a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y_{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"11\" /> are the elementery roots of the Pell equation.\n</p><p>Q.E.D.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Continued_fractions\">Continued fractions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pell_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Continued fractions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The solutions to the Pell equation when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is not a perfect square are connected to the continued <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fraction\" title=\"Fraction\">fraction</a> expansion of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a57557ab311d477de6e7d019f39d4bac82f1f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Period\" title=\"Period\">period</a> of the continued fraction and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/4/d34ed3f59aa127ff4cd30178d970b617e3d05e87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_k=P_k/Q_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" /> is the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />th convergent, all solutions to the Pell equation are in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/5/b7548aeeb99102015d951dbfaa1fbabb307cd7fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(P_{ia},Q_{ia})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" /> for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Positive\">positive</a> integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Generalization\">Generalization</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pell_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Generalization\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A <b>Pell-like equation</b> is a diophantine equation of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/8/188286b01d78bc968152e941f915c33df1261bc0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 - Dy^2 = k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is a natural number and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is an integer. \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Introductory_Problems\">Introductory Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pell_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Introductory Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Show that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are the solutions to the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/f/2bfb0597ace1077ff545ca56072ff41958363a67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 - 2y^2 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/5/305c805e56baba65d2a01c90b5b19e7cf9f89680.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\mid xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Intermediate_Problems\">Intermediate Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pell_equation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Intermediate Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>Find the largest integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> satisfying the following conditions:</li></ul>\n<dl><dd>(i) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/0/6006bba637eef443381fbaff768fa62d9ff41e62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"17\" /> can be expressed as the difference of two consecutive cubes;</dd>\n<dd>(ii) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/6/786204fb628fb98f7452ddf77aa0362194dadf75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n + 79$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> is a perfect square. (<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2008 AIME II Problems/Problem 15\">Source</a>)</dd></dl>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017224502\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.037 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.069 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 371/1000000\nPost‐expand